# Example B1 with a neutron beam and NeutronGeneralProc switched off - the state this port's
# neutron is in until P8 wires the sub-processes and P2 supplies their cross sections.
#
# ONE inactivation, and that is the whole point of the macro. `ref/oracle/neutron_processes.csv`
# says the neutron in QBBC 11.1.1 carries exactly three processes:
#
#     Transportation        type 1  subtype 91
#     Decay                 type 6  subtype 201
#     NeutronGeneralProc    type 4  subtype 116
#
# With `EnableNeutronGeneralProcess = 1` the elastic, inelastic and capture processes are
# sub-processes inside that one, and `G4NeutronTrackingCut::ConstructProcess` returns before it
# can create a `G4NeutronKiller`. So `/process/inactivate hadElastic` names nothing a neutron
# has, `/process/inactivate nCapture` names nothing, and `/process/inactivate nKiller` names
# nothing - inactivating the general process is the only way to get the EM-only column for a
# neutron, and it takes the 10 us time cut off with it.
#
# Decay is left ON deliberately: a free neutron's lifetime is 880 s, so at any energy that
# reaches the far side of a 20 cm phantom the decay length is astronomical and the process is
# inert. Inactivating an inert process would make this macro differ from Geant4's default for no
# reason. `species_tables.csv` carries the lifetime if anyone wants to check that claim.
#
# WHAT THIS SHOULD SHOW. A neutron with no cross section is a G4Transportation-only track: it
# crosses the world in a straight line and leaves with its full kinetic energy, depositing
# nothing anywhere. B1's scoring volume should read exactly zero, and that zero is a real
# prediction rather than an absence - the same run with the process ACTIVE deposits, which is
# what makes it a check. Run this pair and the difference is the whole of the neutron's hadronic
# physics for this geometry.
#
# The port's side: `species_disposition` reports the neutron as stepped, `step_neutral` is handed
# a null table, and its cross section is zero. `TransportEngine::Upload` refuses a table without
# final states, so this cannot silently become a half-wired run.
#
# **P8d: THAT LAST PARAGRAPH IS HISTORY AND THIS MACRO IS NOW STAGE 0 FOR THE PORT AS WELL AS
# FOR GEANT4.** `step_neutral` is handed real tables in every run whose G4PARTICLEXSDATA
# resolves, so the port's half of THIS pair is no longer a zero and the two sides no longer
# agree: running it against the port measures the whole of the neutron's hadronic physics, which
# is what the header above says the difference between the two Geant4 runs is. The pair to use
# for a like-for-like is `stage1_neutron.mac` + `stage1_neutron_port.mac`, through
# `ref/b1neutron/run.bat`. This file is kept because its prediction - a neutron with no cross
# section streams and deposits exactly nothing - is still what `tests/test_neutron.cu` asserts
# with a null table, and because the EM-only column for a neutron is still what it produces on
# the Geant4 side.

/run/initialize
/control/verbose 0
/run/verbose 0

/process/inactivate NeutronGeneralProc

/gun/particle neutron
/gun/energy 100 MeV

/run/printProgress 2000
/run/beamOn 10000
